examples.question_text:
A triangular section $OPQ$ is surveyed from $O$. The true bearings of $P$ and $Q$ are $330^{\circ}$ and $050^{\circ}$ respectively. Find (a) its area and (b) its perimeter, each correct to $1$ decimal place.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (P) at (120:2);
\coordinate (Q) at (40:2.5);
\draw[->,dashed] (O)--(0,2.7);
\path (0,2.7) node[above] {$N$};
\draw (O)--(P)--(Q)--cycle;
\path (O)--(P) node[midway,left] {$40\text{ m}$};
\path (O)--(Q) node[midway,below right] {$50\text{ m}$};
\path (P) node[above left] {$P\;(330^{\circ})$};
\path (Q) node[right] {$Q\;(050^{\circ})$};
\path (O) node[below] {$O$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
The angle crosses north.
$\begin{aligned}\angle POQ&=(360^{\circ}-330^{\circ})+50^{\circ}\\&=80^{\circ}\end{aligned}$
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (P) at (120:2);
\coordinate (Q) at (40:2.5);
\draw[->,dashed] (O)--(0,2.7);
\path (0,2.7) node[above] {$N$};
\draw (O)--(P)--(Q)--cycle;
\path (O)--(P) node[midway,left] {$40\text{ m}$};
\path (O)--(Q) node[midway,below right] {$50\text{ m}$};
\draw (40:0.55) arc[start angle=40,end angle=120,radius=0.55];
\path (80:0.85) node {$80^{\circ}$};
\path (P) node[above left] {$P$};
\path (Q) node[right] {$Q$};
\path (O) node[below] {$O$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
(a) Use the two radials and their included angle.
$\begin{aligned}A&=\tfrac12(40)(50)\sin80^{\circ}\\&=984.807753\ldots\\&\approx984.8\text{ m}^{2}\end{aligned}$
(b) Find the unknown boundary using the cosine rule.
$\begin{aligned}PQ&=\sqrt{40^{2}+50^{2}-2(40)(50)\cos80^{\circ}}\\&=58.355867\ldots\text{ m}\end{aligned}$
Both radials are external edges of this triangular section.
$\begin{aligned}P_{\text{section}}&=40+50+58.355867\ldots\\&\approx148.4\text{ m}\end{aligned}$

examples.question_text:
A triangular field $ABC$ is surveyed from an interior point $O$. Find all angles at $O$, check that they add to $360^{\circ}$, and calculate the total area in hectares, correct to $2$ decimal places. All distances shown are in metres; $1\text{ ha}=10\,000\text{ m}^{2}$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (A) at (50:2.4);
\coordinate (B) at (-50:2.7);
\coordinate (C) at (-170:2.1);
\draw[->,dashed] (O)--(0,3);
\path (0,3) node[above] {$N$};
\draw (O)--(A) (O)--(B) (O)--(C);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--cycle;
\path (O)--(A) node[midway,above left] {$80$};
\path (O)--(B) node[midway,above right] {$90$};
\path (O)--(C) node[midway,above] {$70$};
\path (A) node[above right] {$A\;(040^{\circ})$};
\path (B) node[below right] {$B\;(140^{\circ})$};
\path (C) node[left] {$C\;(260^{\circ})$};
\path (O) node[below left] {$O$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
Calculate the angles between consecutive radials.
$\begin{aligned}\angle AOB&=140^{\circ}-40^{\circ}=100^{\circ}\\\angle BOC&=260^{\circ}-140^{\circ}=120^{\circ}\\\angle COA&=(360^{\circ}-260^{\circ})+40^{\circ}=140^{\circ}\end{aligned}$
The triangles surround $O$ completely: $100^{\circ}+120^{\circ}+140^{\circ}=360^{\circ}$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (A) at (50:2.4);
\coordinate (B) at (-50:2.7);
\coordinate (C) at (-170:2.1);
\draw (O)--(A) (O)--(B) (O)--(C);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--cycle;
\path (O)--(A) node[pos=0.7,above left] {$80$};
\path (O)--(B) node[pos=0.7,above right] {$90$};
\path (O)--(C) node[pos=0.7,above] {$70$};
\draw (-50:0.45) arc[start angle=-50,end angle=50,radius=0.45];
\draw (-170:0.55) arc[start angle=-170,end angle=-50,radius=0.55];
\draw (50:0.65) arc[start angle=50,end angle=190,radius=0.65];
\path (0:0.9) node {$100^{\circ}$};
\path (-110:0.95) node {$120^{\circ}$};
\path (120:1) node {$140^{\circ}$};
\path (A) node[above] {$A$};
\path (B) node[below right] {$B$};
\path (C) node[left] {$C$};
\path (O) node[below left] {$O$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Find each triangle's area, keeping unrounded values for the total.
$\begin{aligned}A_{AOB}&=\tfrac12(80)(90)\sin100^{\circ}=3545.307910\ldots\text{ m}^{2}\\A_{BOC}&=\tfrac12(90)(70)\sin120^{\circ}=2727.980021\ldots\text{ m}^{2}\\A_{COA}&=\tfrac12(70)(80)\sin140^{\circ}=1799.805307\ldots\text{ m}^{2}\end{aligned}$
$\begin{aligned}A_{\text{total}}&=A_{AOB}+A_{BOC}+A_{COA}\\&=8073.093239\ldots\text{ m}^{2}\\A_{\text{total in ha}}&=8073.093239\ldots\div10\,000\\&\approx0.81\text{ ha}\end{aligned}$

examples.question_text:
A field $DEF$ is surveyed from an interior point $O$. Calculate the length of fencing required around the field, correct to $1$ decimal place. All distances shown are in metres.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (D) at (50:2);
\coordinate (E) at (-50:2.5);
\coordinate (F) at (-170:1.5);
\draw[->,dashed] (O)--(0,2.8);
\path (0,2.8) node[above] {$N$};
\draw (O)--(D) (O)--(E) (O)--(F);
\draw (D)--(E)--(F)--cycle;
\path (O)--(D) node[midway,above left] {$40$};
\path (O)--(E) node[midway,above right] {$50$};
\path (O)--(F) node[midway,above] {$30$};
\path (D) node[above right] {$D\;(040^{\circ})$};
\path (E) node[below right] {$E\;(140^{\circ})$};
\path (F) node[left] {$F\;(260^{\circ})$};
\path (O) node[below left] {$O$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
$\begin{aligned}\angle DOE&=140^{\circ}-40^{\circ}=100^{\circ}\\\angle EOF&=260^{\circ}-140^{\circ}=120^{\circ}\\\angle FOD&=(360^{\circ}-260^{\circ})+40^{\circ}=140^{\circ}\end{aligned}$
The angles close: $100^{\circ}+120^{\circ}+140^{\circ}=360^{\circ}$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (D) at (50:2);
\coordinate (E) at (-50:2.5);
\coordinate (F) at (-170:1.5);
\draw (O)--(D) (O)--(E) (O)--(F);
\draw (D)--(E)--(F)--cycle;
\path (O)--(D) node[pos=0.8,above left] {$40$};
\path (O)--(E) node[pos=0.8,above right] {$50$};
\path (O)--(F) node[pos=0.85,above] {$30$};
\draw (-50:0.4) arc[start angle=-50,end angle=50,radius=0.4];
\draw (-170:0.45) arc[start angle=-170,end angle=-50,radius=0.45];
\draw (50:0.5) arc[start angle=50,end angle=190,radius=0.5];
\path (0:0.8) node {$100^{\circ}$};
\path (-110:0.8) node {$120^{\circ}$};
\path (120:0.85) node {$140^{\circ}$};
\path (D) node[above] {$D$};
\path (E) node[below right] {$E$};
\path (F) node[left] {$F$};
\path (O) node[below left] {$O$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Use the cosine rule for each external side.
$\begin{aligned}DE&=\sqrt{40^{2}+50^{2}-2(40)(50)\cos100^{\circ}}\\&=69.242997\ldots\text{ m}\\EF&=\sqrt{50^{2}+30^{2}-2(50)(30)\cos120^{\circ}}\\&=70\text{ m}\\FD&=\sqrt{30^{2}+40^{2}-2(30)(40)\cos140^{\circ}}\\&=65.867341\ldots\text{ m}\end{aligned}$
The radials are internal survey lines, so exclude them from the fencing length.
$\begin{aligned}P&=DE+EF+FD\\&=69.242997\ldots+70+65.867341\ldots\\&\approx205.1\text{ m}\end{aligned}$

examples.question_text:
A radial survey gives $OB=30\text{ m}$, $OC=25\text{ m}$ and a true bearing of $120^{\circ}$ for $B$ from $O$. Triangle $BOC$ has area $300\text{ m}^{2}$ and its angle at $O$ is acute. The diagram shows $C$ clockwise from $B$. Find the bearing of $C$ from $O$, to the nearest degree.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (-30:2.4);
\coordinate (C) at (-83.130102:2);
\draw[->,dashed] (O)--(0,1.6);
\path (0,1.6) node[above] {$N$};
\draw (O)--(B)--(C)--cycle;
\path (O)--(B) node[midway,above right] {$30\text{ m}$};
\path (O)--(C) node[midway,left] {$25\text{ m}$};
\path (B) node[right] {$B\;(120^{\circ})$};
\path (C) node[below] {$C$};
\path (O) node[left] {$O$};
\path (1.2,-1.1) node {$300\text{ m}^{2}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
Rearrange the triangle area rule, then take the acute inverse-sine solution.
$\begin{aligned}A&=\tfrac12ab\sin C\\300&=\tfrac12(30)(25)\sin\angle BOC\\\sin\angle BOC&=\frac{300}{375}=0.8\\\angle BOC&=\sin^{-1}(0.8)\\&=53.130102\ldots^{\circ}\end{aligned}$
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (-30:2.4);
\coordinate (C) at (-83.130102:2);
\draw[->,dashed] (O)--(0,1.6);
\path (0,1.6) node[above] {$N$};
\draw (O)--(B)--(C)--cycle;
\path (O)--(B) node[pos=0.65,above right,xshift=4mm,yshift=3mm] {$30\text{ m}$};
\path (O)--(C) node[pos=0.75,left] {$25\text{ m}$};
\draw (-83.130102:0.55) arc[start angle=-83.130102,end angle=-30,radius=0.55];
\path (1.1,-2.9) node {$53.1301\ldots^{\circ}$};
\path (B) node[right] {$B\;(120^{\circ})$};
\path (C) node[below] {$C$};
\path (O) node[left] {$O$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Since $C$ lies clockwise from $B$, add the angle to the bearing of $B$.
$\begin{aligned}\text{Bearing of }C&=120^{\circ}+53.130102\ldots^{\circ}\\&\approx173^{\circ}\text{T}\end{aligned}$
